Maximum Depth of Binary Tree (LeetCode 104)
Problem Overview
Find the maximum depth (height) of a binary tree, defined as the number of nodes along the longest path from the root down to the farthest leaf.
Intuition & Mental ModelThink:
Think bottom-up recursion (Post-Order Traversal: Left $\rightarrow$ Right $\rightarrow$ Root).
A null node has a height of 0
The height of any node is 1 + max(height of left subtree, height of right subtree).
Optimal approach:
Base Case: If root == nullptr, return 0.
Recurse Left & Right: Compute the height of the left and right subtrees.
Combine: Take the maximum of both subtrees and add 1 for the current node.
public:
    int maxDepth(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) return 0;

        int lh = maxDepth(root->left);
        int rh = maxDepth(root->right);

        return 1 + max(lh, rh);
    }
};
Time Complexity: $O(N)$ — Visits every node exactly once.
Space Complexity: $O(H)$ — Call stack space ($O(\log N)$ for balanced trees, $O(N)$ for skewed trees).
